Here's an implementation of a function in R that concatenates two strings of a given length using only bitwise operations:

```R
concatenate_strings <- function(str1, str2, length) {
  result <- 0
  for (i in 1:length) {
    if (i <= nchar(str1)) {
      result <- bitwShiftL(result, 8) + as.numeric(charToRaw(substr(str1, i, i)))
    } else {
      result <- bitwShiftL(result, 8)
    }

    if (i <= nchar(str2)) {
      result <- bitwShiftL(result, 8) + as.numeric(charToRaw(substr(str2, i, i)))
    } else {
      result <- bitwShiftL(result, 8)
    }
  }

  return(rawToChar(intToRaw(result)))
}
```

This implementation uses a loop to iterate over each character position in the desired length of the resulting string. It checks if the current position is within the length of each input string and then concatenates the ASCII representation of the characters using bitwise shift and addition operations.

Note that the implementation assumes that the input strings are in ASCII format.

The time complexity of this implementation is O(log n) because the number of iterations in the loop is proportional to the length of the longer input string, which has a logarithmic relationship with n.

