Here's a JavaScript function that checks if a string ends with a specific substring, considering case sensitivity and whitespace characters:

```javascript
function endsWith(str, substr) {
  // Trim any whitespace characters from the end of the string
  str = str.trimEnd();

  // Check if the substring length is greater than the string length
  if (substr.length > str.length) {
    return false;
  }

  // Get the end part of the string that matches the substring length
  var endPart = str.substr(-substr.length);

  // Compare the end part with the substring
  return endPart === substr;
}
```

You can use this function as follows:

```javascript
console.log(endsWith("Hello World", "rld")); // true
console.log(endsWith("Hello World", "Rld")); // false (case-sensitive)
console.log(endsWith("Hello World", "orld")); // false (missing 'W')
console.log(endsWith("Hello World   ", "   ")); // true (consider whitespace characters)
console.log(endsWith("Hello World", "World")); // false (missing 'W' and case-sensitive)
```

